{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '105,138p' vendor/engine/API.md && sed -n '210,245p' vendor/engine/API.md && cat vendor/engine/docs/ASSETS.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '105,138p' vendor/engine/API.md && sed -n '210,245p' vendor/engine/API.md && cat vendor/engine/docs/ASSETS.md\"","aggregated_output":"\n## Scenes (title, play, game over)\n```js\ngame.start({ scene: 'title', scenes: {\n  title: { update(dt) { if (game.input.pressed('start')) game.go('play', { level: 1 }); },\n           draw(r) { r.sky(['#101848', '#402a70']); r.text('PRESS START', r.W / 2, 100, '#fff', { align: 'center', scale: 2 }); } },\n  play: { pausable: true, view: 'side', input: 'PLATFORMER', res: 'snes', views: ['side', 'brawler'], touch: ['jump', 'attack'],\n          enter(data) { /* build the level */ }, exit() {}, update(dt) {}, draw(r) {} }\n} });\n```\n`game.go(name, data)` switches after a short dithered fade (`game.fadeTime`: a test advances past it; `{ fade: 0 }` cuts; `{ fade: 'wipe' | 'iris' | 'mosaic' | 'swirl' | 'dissolve' | 'white', seconds: .4, at: hero }` a transition, a plain fade under reduce motion); timers (but `after(s, fn, { keep: true })`) and particles are cleared, a hit-stop ends (`timeScale` stays). `pausable: true`: Escape / P / Start pauses it, with the pause menu (below). `view`, `views`, `input`, `res`, `expand`, `touch`, `camera`, `propSize` are options applied on entry (a method so named warns). A scene is one object every visit: reset its state in `enter`. `back()` takes a phone's back gesture first (true if it went back: a sub-menu closed).\n\n## Motion and timing: tweens, scripts, paths, weather\n- Tweens (Phaser's): `game.tween(door, { y: 40 }, .5, { ease: 'outBack', delay, repeat, yoyo, onDone, keep })` eases numbers on game time: paused with the game, slowed by `timeScale`, ended by a scene change unless `keep`. Returns `{ done, cancel() }`. `E.ease` has Penner's set: `linear`, `inSine` `outSine` `inOutSine`, `inQuad` `outQuad` `inOutQuad`, the same for `Cubic`, `Back`, `Elastic`, `Bounce`.\n- Scripts (Unity's coroutines) for cutscenes, boss patterns, wave timelines, tutorials:\n```js full-script\ngame.script(function* () {\n  yield 1;                                   // seconds of game time\n  yield E.until(() => door.open);            // a condition\n  yield game.tween(boss, { y: 80 }, 1.5);    // a tween (or another script, or a generator) finishing\n  for (let w = 0; w < 3; w++) { spawnWave(w); yield E.any([8, () => enemies.length === 0]); }\n});\n```\n  `E.all([...])` waits for all; a bare `yield` waits one step. It runs at once to its first `yield`, the same at any refresh rate; `{ keep: true }` outlives the scene; `.cancel()`, `.done`, `.value`.\n- Paths: `const p = new E.Path([[x, y, z], ...], { closed, smooth: 'catmull' })`: `p.length`, `p.at(d)` (`{ x, y, z, angle }` by distance along: moving platforms, dives, rails), `p.nearest(x, y)` (a racer's progress), `p.draw(r)` to see it.\n- Weather: `game.weather = { kind: 'rain' | 'snow' | 'ash' | 'leaves' | 'sand', amount: .8, wind: .3, lightning: .2 }` (null: clear) falls through the world in every view, settles or splashes, flashes and thunders; cosmetic only (its own random stream), and it ends with the scene: set it in `enter`.\n- Wrap-around (Asteroids): `x = E.wrap(x, 0, W)`, `E.wrapDiff(a, b, W)` (the short way across the seam), and `wrap: [W, H]` on `r.sprite` or `r.actor` draws a copy across each edge.\n\n## Standard features (every game has them: don't build your own)\nWith no code: a **pause menu** on pausable scenes (Resume, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Quit to title; keys, mouse, gamepad and touch), **settings** saved per game (`game.settings`: volume, music, effects, mute, reduce motion, shake and flashes, pixel scaling, a screen filter, the frame rate and its readout), **controls** the player rebinds (conflicts said first; dead zone, invert aim and move, button symbols, vibration; touch button side, size, opacity), **full screen** (a phone's first tap, a top-right button, the menu; turning it off is remembered), a **menu button** at the top right (on a touch screen it stays in play: it is the touch START), a **way out** of every menu screen, the **back gesture** stepping back in the game (`game.back()`) before it leaves the page, **pausing** when the page is hidden or the controller unplugs, and a page set up for phones (safe areas, no scrolling, zooming or pull to refresh on the canvas).\n- Adjust: `new E.Game({ menu: { items: [['Restart', fn]], settings: [{ key: 'hints', label: 'Hints', value: true }, { key: 'difficulty', options: ['Easy', 'Normal', 'Hard'], value: 'Normal' }], controls: { attack: 'Sword', skill: false }, quit: 'title' } })`, then read `game.settings.hints`. Leave parts out: `menu: false` (your own pause), `fullscreen: 'button' | false`, `corner: false`, `backGesture: false`, `autoPause: false`, `touch: 'never'` (a phone is told a controller or keyboard is needed). `game.cornerArea()`: where the corner's buttons cover the screen (or null): keep a HUD clear of it.\n- Save slots (`game.saves`): three slots and an autosave, each with its place, level and time played. `game.saves.write = () => ({ data, place, level })` and `read = save => {...}`; then `save(slot)` (0, 1, 2 or `'auto'`), `load(slot)`, `copy(a, b)`, `erase(slot)`, `autosave()`, `list()`, `latest()` (a title's Continue). `menu: { saves: true }` (or `{ slots, version, migrate(data, from) }`) puts Save (while `saves.canSave`: a save point) and Load in the pause menu, and `saves.show('save' | 'load')` opens the screen from your own menu. Blocked storage keeps them for the visit.\n- Prompts: `game.input.label('jump')` is that action's key or button on the player's device, after rebinding ('Space', 'A', '✕'). PlayStation symbols by default: `settings: [{ key: 'glyphs', value: 'playstation' }]` (a standard key without a label sets its default). A canvas title's OPTIONS: `game.menu('settings')`. From code: `game.settings.set(key, v)`, `game.fullscreen.toggle()` (from a key, click or tap), `game.pause(reason)`, `game.resume()`. `live: true` (or `game.menu(name, { pause: false })`): menus over a world that keeps stepping (online play).\n- **Boot**: heavy setup in steps behind a boot screen, and a report for any error before play: `game.start({ scenes, scene, boot: { title, steps: [['Building the city', () => { map = build(); }]] } })` (`AI_GUIDE.md`, Boot).\n  - kicks: `kick roundhouse sweep flyingkick knee axekick`;\n  - other: `cast` (both hands) `throw bash claw`.\n  Give each character its own set (a knight: `overhead`, `thrust`, `spin`; a mage: `cast`; a monk: `jab`, `cross`, `roundhouse`; a boss: `haymaker`, `sweep`). `E.move('uppercut', .5)` is the attack state of any move at any moment (previews, cutscenes).\n- `attack: { spec, phase: 'wind' | 'active' | 'recover', u: 0..1 }`, spec `{ a0, a1, z0, z1, reach, r0, r1, hand, rel, blade, spin, kick, plane, lunge, hop, crouch, lean, twist, hold }`. Angles are relative to facing (+ = right hand side). `rel: true`: heights relative to the shoulder and reach scaled by arm length (kick heights as a share of hip height). `r0`/`r1`: reach that extends (thrusts, jabs). `hand: 'L' | 'both'`. In side views horizontal swings turn into the screen plane (a Castlevania chop) by themselves; `plane: 'side' | 'ground'` (or the rig option `swingPlane`) chooses. `lunge` steps into the strike (body motion in local units, clamped to -4..5; to move the character itself, add speed to your body), `hop` lifts the body, `crouch` dips in the wind-up, `hold` is how long the end pose holds (share of recover). You time the phases.\n- In steep ground views (threequarter) rigs and Blobs are drawn from a lower, sprite-like angle so faces show, like SNES RPG sprites (`charView: false` turns it off for one rig, `E.style.charPitch = false` for all).\n- Faces (eyes with catch-lights, brows, mouth), a cheated 3/4 turn toward the camera in side views (`cheat: .5`, 0 turns it off) and hair layers are automatic. `face: { eyes: 'big' (anime / Mana style), bangs: 0..1 }`.\n- Poses: `rig.update(dt, { ..., pose: 'cheer' | 'cast' | 'guard' | 'kneel' | 'crouch' | 'wave' | 'hips' | 'down' })` (they blend in and out), `'block'` (weapon upright across the body), `'die'` (a stagger, then the body topples), `expr: 'smile' | 'shout' | 'angry' | 'wince'` (faces and portraits), `run: 0..1` (run in place), or `down: 0..1` to lie a defeated body flat. `stance: 'guard'` keeps fists up while walking (brawlers; the free hand guards the chin during punches), `stance: 'ready'` holds the weapon forward. `climb: true` with `vz` climbs a ladder hand over hand (`Platformer.rigState()` passes both). Traversal: `crouch` (a sneaking gait), `sprint` (a lean), `hang` with `hangZ` (hands on an edge that high above the feet), `flip: 0..1` (a roll), `land: k` (a heavy landing); `mover.rigState()` sets them. Every wind-up starts from the rig's current pose, so chained hits and hand changes never snap. Idle rigs breathe and shift their weight by themselves.\n- `rig.draw(g, ox, oy, view)` inside `r.actor`, `rig.drawSmear(r)` (a glowing trail along the real blade, fist or foot path, in every view), `rig.debug(r)` (skeleton), `rig.kick(v)` (squash and stretch), `rig.hand()` and `rig.tip()` (world points to spawn bullets from), `rig.worldOffset(p)` (where a point in the rig's own frame, such as a joint in `rig.J`, sits from its feet: to draw or spawn at any joint), `rig.lastView` (the view it last drew with), `rig.solvePose()` (sets `rig.J` from its state each step: wrap it on one rig to move joints).\n**Blob** (slimes, rabbites, bats, imps, round monsters): `new E.Blob({ R: 6, colors: { dk, base, lt, spec, eye, pupil }, ears: 'rabbit' | 'cat', horns: true, wings: true, feet: true, tail: true, mouth: true | 'fangs', face: 'front' })`, `update(dt, { squash: -0.4..0.4, look: [dx, dy], squint, walk: 0..1, flap: 0..1 (wing speed; 0 folds them), hang: true (roost upside down) })`, `kick(v)`, `draw(g, ox, oy, view)`. Mix the parts for a bestiary: `wings` + `mouth: 'fangs'` is a bat, `ears: 'rabbit'` + `feet` a Mana rabbite, `horns` + `tail` an imp.\n\n## Effects and lighting\n- Particles: `game.particles.explosion(x, y, z, size)` (fireball, smoke, sparks, debris, ring, shake, sound), `fire(x, y, z, n, { size })`, `smoke(x, y, z, n, { size, color })`, `glints(x, y, z, n, color)`, `dust(x, y, z, n)`, `sparks(x, y, z, n, angle)`, `bits(x, y, z, n, colors)`, `ring(x, y, r0, r1, color, dur)`, `text(x, y, z, '12', color, { scale: 2, bounce: true })` (bouncing damage numbers), `add({ kind: 'dust' | 'spark' | 'bit' | 'ember' | 'fire' | 'smoke' | 'glint', ... })`. `explosion(x, y, z, size, { debris, freeze: false, shake: 0 })` for chains of small blasts. Side-scrollers: `particles.ground = (x, y, z) => level.groundBelow(x, z)` so sparks and debris bounce on platforms. Screen flash: `game.flash('#ffffff', .12, strength 0..1)`.\n- Lighting (step 9; every view, side-scrollers included, no WebGPU needed): `game.lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity, { color: '#ffa050' })` every draw. Dark areas get a smooth cool tint, lights add their own color, and emissive items (flames, sparks, bullets, `emissive: true`) stay bright. `lights.darkness` (0..1) and `lights.tint` tune it; after setting `lights.dark = [r, g, b]`, `levels` or `glow`, call `lights.refresh()`. Props with flames can light the scene: `r.prop('torch', x, y, z, { light: true })`. Lamps, street lamps, lanterns and crystals glow on their own (`haloR`, `haloColor`, `halo: false`); `cone: true` adds a light cone under a street lamp.\n- Hits: `game.hitFx(x, y, z, { power: 1, angle, damage: 12, textScale: 2, textZ: 22 })` does hit-stop, shake, an impact star, sparks, a bouncing damage number (`bounce: false` floats it) and a sound in one call; `particles.impact(x, y, z, size, color)` for the star alone.\n- GPU lighting (optional, ground views with a TileMap): `const gpu = game.enableGPU({ map })`; mark glowing things with `px.glow(g, 1)`; add `lights.caster(x, y, r, top)` and `lights.heat(x, y, z, w)`. Where it can't draw (no WebGPU, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with `game.lights.ambient` (`gpu.fallback = false`: unlit).\n\n## Genre playbook (NES, SNES, Genesis, N64, PS1)\n| Genre (examples) | View | Build it with | Copy slice |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Platformer (Mario, Mega Man, Sonic, Castlevania) | `side` (+ `brawler` for 2.5D) | PlatformMap, Platformer, Humanoid, sprites, Bullets `'side'` | PLATFORMER |\n| Metroidvania (Metroid, Symphony of the Night) | `side` | an `E.World` of PlatformMaps (the rooms module: doors at open edges, a slide, the explored map, gates opened by abilities, saves), a Platformer given `airJumps` and `dash` as they are found, its further moves, an arm cannon aimed eight ways (`point`, `aim`, `rig.tip()`, Bullets) | METROIDVANIA |\n| Action adventure (Zelda, Secret of Mana) | `threequarter` or `topdown` | TileMap rows, `cam.room`, Body, Humanoid + `E.Attack` sword, Dialog, `E.ui.hearts` | ADVENTURE |\n| Point-and-click adventure (Monkey Island, Day of the Tentacle, Broken Sword) | none: rooms painted in screen pixels (`r.overlay`) | `E.Click` (the click module): rooms with walk areas and holes (`E.Walkbox`), depth scale, hotspots, the nine verbs, Sierra's icons or a verb coin, an inventory, puzzles as rules; talk through `E.Story` | POINT-AND-CLICK |\n| Visual novel (Phoenix Wright, Snatcher, Clannad) | none | `E.Story` nodes and choices over a painted backdrop, `rig.drawPortrait` at size, `story.log` (the backlog), `story.auto`, `story.skip`; `game.saves` | POINT-AND-CLICK |\n| Action RPG / dungeon (Diablo, Landstalker) | `iso` | TileMap, FlowField, Body (jump onto blocks), GPU lighting | ADVENTURE |\n| Beat-'em-up (Final Fight, Streets of Rage, TMNT) | `brawler` | TileMap street strip, Body, Humanoid (`weapon: null`) + `E.Combo` punches and `kick: true` kicks, `E.knockback`, waves that lock the screen; a second player drops in (`game.allowJoin`, `cam.frame` on both) | BRAWLER |\n| Fighting (Street Fighter II, Darkstalkers) | `side` | two `E.Fighter`s in an `E.Match` (the fight module): moves with frame data, guards, throws, specials by motion (`'236'`) or charge, a meter, projectiles; `E.FightAI`, `E.FightSelect`, training | FIGHTING |\n| Shoot-'em-up (1942, Gradius, R-Type) | `overhead` (vertical, and horizontal: world x scrolls) | sprites, Bullets, `E.pattern`, a timeline of waves; horizontal: rock edges as functions of x, a power bar, options on the ship's past path, `E.Path` loops; `E.Arcade` for the continue and the table | SHOOTER (both stages) |\n| Pinball (Pinball Fantasies, Space Cadet, Devil's Crush) | a steep table view of its own (`new E.View('pinball', 'Table', 0, 62, 1, 1)`) | `E.Physics`: chains for the walls, `flipper`, `bumper`, `slingshot`, `kicker`, a ramp on a layer of its own, the ball a `bullet`; `input.zones` flip on a phone; `E.Arcade` for the score and table. Breakout and Asteroids with real collisions are its recipes | PINBALL |\n| Artillery (Worms, Scorched Earth) | `side` at a whole scale (`new E.View(id, label, 0, 0, 1, 1)`) | `E.Terrain` (`carve` the blasts, `walk` the soldiers, `ray` the shots), grenades as `E.Physics` bodies on `land.collider()`, `E.Turns`; a drag aims (`steer = 'drag'`, `drag()`, `stickOn`); a CPU tries its shots in simulation | ARTILLERY |\n| Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids) | `overhead` (one fixed screen, cabinet art on the sides) | `E.Arcade` (score, lives, extra lives, turns, initials, tables, attract mode); a maze of `E.GridMover`s; moths on `E.Path`s turned by `rot`; `E.wrap` and `wrap: [w, h]` | ARCADE |\n| Twin-stick / run-and-gun (Smash TV, Contra) | `topdown` / `side` | Body or Platformer, Bullets, `weapon: 'gun'`, `point: true` | SHOOTER + ADVENTURE |\n| JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy, EarthBound) | `threequarter` town, `brawler` battles | TileMap towns and a big world map, the story and rpg modules, Dialog with markup, `game.saves` slots | RPG |\n| Puzzle (Tetris, Dr. Mario, Puyo, Columns) | `side` (a field in screen pixels) | a cell array, a bag from `E.rng`, `input.repeat` (auto-shift), `grid.groups` (chains), `input.drag()` and `clicked()` for touch; versus: two fields, rows sent across (`game.players`) | PUZZLE |\n| Roguelike (Rogue, Mystery Dungeon, Shiren) | `threequarter` / `iso` | `E.gen` floors, a TileMap of its rows, `E.Grid.from(map)` paths and `fov`, `E.Fog`, `E.Turns` by speed, `game.tween` steps | ROGUELIKE |\n| Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Tactics Ogre, Fire Emblem) | `iso`, turned a quarter | TileMap of block heights, `map.pick` under the cursor, `E.Grid.from(map, { tops: true })` reach over heights, `grid.shape`, `grid.side`, `grid.threat`, `E.Turns` charge time, `E.Story` | TACTICS |\n# Objects and materials as data\n\nThe full engine draws still objects (barrels, tables, trees, wells) from a few lines of data, in every view and facing,\nwith the house shading and outline, and gives every surface (floors, wall faces, wall tops, platform tiles, the objects'\nown faces) a material from one open registry. An object costs one to a few hundred tokens of data (the core's median is\n125); you check it headless with one command. Read this card whole before you write one. (The agent edition keeps the\nregistries and ignores the calls: `E.addAssets` returns empty lists, the others null.)\n\n## Use one\n\n```js\n// in draw(r): feet on the point, depth sorted like an actor; its front toward facing\nr.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing: Math.PI / 2 });\n// once, before the updates: bodies stop at it, a body coming down lands on it, one wholly below passes under\nmap.solid('table', 120, 80, 0, { facing: 0 });\n// a sprite of it, for an inventory or a HUD (r.sprite, px.sprite)\nconst icon = E.shapeSprite('chest', { view: 'iso' });\n```\n\n- **r.shape options:** facing (radians, where the front points: 0 east, π/2 south, as written), scale, mats ({ slot:\n  material }), flip (mirrors it x to -x, collision too), params (a generator's: { h: 30 }), bias, hides (default true),\n  light (adds its lights to game.lights), shadow (default true).\n- **map.solid** takes the same id and placement, so one object can feed both calls. Bodies span z to z + h (E.Body's h, 32).\n- **In 3D** the 3D engine draws the same entries, materials and boxes: `world.shape(id, x, y, z, o)` (its header).\n- **Steep views** (top-down) draw shapes from the characters' lower angle, as rigs are drawn. The light comes from the\n  viewer's upper left in every view.\n- **Glow and fire:** a material with glow: 1 ignores the light and stays bright in the dark; an entry's flames ([[x, y, z,\n  w, h]]) draw the engine's animated flame (r.shape only: a sprite has none). Anything else that moves stays a prop\n  (r.prop) or your own code.\n- **Dressing a generated level:** a scene is a few entries as [id, across, forward] from an anchor (a wall spot, forward\n  into the room); place it whole or not at all, each part on free floor, after the level's own random picks and from a\n  seeded stream of its own, so the level stays as its seed builds it (Emberdeep's `src/emberdeep/53-scenes.js`).\n\n## The core library\n\n**Shapes** (sized for a 26-unit hero; generators take params): barrel, crate, chest, table, chair, stool, bench, bed,\nbookcase, shelf, pot, jar, sack, cart, well, sign, lamppost, anvil; fence { len: 16 }, pine { h: 41, tiers: 4 },\ntree { h: 40, seed: 1 }, bush { r: 7 }, grass { h: 6, n: 6 }, rock { r: 7.5, seed: 2 }, crystal { h: 18, seed: 1 }.\n`r.shape('pine', x, y, 0, { params: { h: 30 } })`; `E.SHAPES.barrel.about` says what each is.\n\n**Materials:** woods oak walnut birch crate plank planks wood; stones stone cobble marble granite sandstone slate rock\nbrick flagstone ruin; metals iron steel gold copper bronze metal rusted; clay glazed porcelain; cloth linen burlap wool velvet leather;\nplants bark needles leaves shrub moss straw hedge grass; ground dirt sand snow mud; water ice crystal flame bone shingle;\nwalls timber plaster; tops speckle plain tiles slab; tiles block bonus ground pipe checker.\n\n## Materials on surfaces\n\n```js\nE.MATERIALS.mossy = { color: '#6a7a5a', pattern: 'stones', size: 6 };   // or the shorthand '#6a7a5a stones size=6'\nconst types = { 1: { h: 32, top: '#6a6280', side: '#4a4360', face: 'cobble', roof: 'slate' } };   // TileMap walls\nconst map = new E.TileMap({ rows, legend, types, floorMat: 'flagstone', floorMats: { water: 'water' } });   // floors\nconst level = new E.PlatformMap({ rows, legend, types: { 1: { kind: 'solid', style: 'block', mat: 'mossy' } } });\n```\n\nfloorMats is keyed by the floor tags of the legend ('~': { floor: 'water' }); either stands in for floorTex.\n\nA material is `{ color, pattern, ...the pattern's numbers, ramp, glow, over }`. Its five tones come from `E.tones(color)`\n(or its own `ramp` of five, dark to light); the light picks a band, the pattern moves it a step, so a material works on\nevery surface and in every palette and recolors with one hex. An unknown name warns with the list of names.\nOverlays, the last on top, each in its own color's tones: `over: [['moss', '#5f7a3a', { up: .6 }], ['cracks']]`; moss on\nwhat looks up, cracks (the material a step darker), dirt near the ground (h 6), rust; each takes amount and size.\n\n| Pattern | Numbers | What |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| none | | the light alone |\n| planks | bw 3, len 14, dir | boards with joints; staves on round parts; dir: 'v' turns them |\n| crate | frame 2, bw 3 | a framed, braced side; planks on its lid |\n| bricks | bw 6, bh 3, mortar | running bond |\n| stones | size 5 | irregular stones (Voronoi), dark joints, a lit rim |\n| tiles | size 4 | square tiles, grout |\n| speck, noise | amount .08; scale 4 | specks; soft patches |\n| foliage | size 3.5 | leaf clumps lit on top |\n| bark | | fissures; rings on a cut end |\n| metal | rivet | a shine where lit; rivets |\n| cloth | fold .9 | weave and folds |\n| glaze | | a glossy shine |\n| thatch, shingles | course; bw | straw courses; roof tiles |\n\nYour own pattern: `E.PATTERNS.waves = (s, m) => Math.sin(s.u / (m.size || 3)) > .8 ? 1 : 0` (s.u, s.v: world units\nacross and up the surface; s.lid, s.curved, s.lit, s.W, s.H; return a step, -1 to +2). A material may also paint one kind\nof surface its own way, per pixel: `face(wx, z, tones, type, cx, cy, fi, nh, h)` or `roof(wx, wy, tones, type)` returning\na color or null, `floor(x, y, tag, view)` returning [r, g, b].\n\n## Write one\n\nUnits: 16 = 1 m = one tile; x east, y south, z up. The origin is the middle of the footprint on the ground; write it\nfacing south (+y). Use whole or half units: a pixel at game size is about 0.7 units.\n\n```js\nE.SHAPES.stool = { about: 'round three-legged stool', tags: 'furniture tavern', mats: { w: 'oak', l: 'walnut' },\n  parts: [['cyl','w',[0,0,8],5,1.5], ['ring',3,3,['box','l',[0,0,0],[1.5,1.5,8],{rot:[0,-8,0]}]]] };\n```\n\nAn entry: `about`, `tags`, `mats` (slot to material; a slot name that is not in mats is a material itself), and `parts`, or\n`make(params)` returning parts with `params` its defaults. Optional: `scale` (times the placement's), `foot: [x0, y0,\nx1, y1]` (the footprint for collision, and for sorting when it stands raised; the bounds by default), `lights: [[x, y,\nz, color, radius]]`, `flames: [[x, y, z, w, h]]`.\n\n| Part | Form | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| box | `['box', m, at, [w, d, h], { rot }]` | at: the bottom middle |\n| cyl | `['cyl', m, at, r, h, { r2, seg, sq }]` | r2: top radius (0: a cone); sq squashes y; seg: 6 for a hexagon |\n| lathe | `['lathe', m, at, [[r, z], ...], { seg }]` | a profile turned about z, bottom to top; [0, z] closes an end |\n| ball | `['ball', m, at, r, { sz }]` | at: its centre; sz below 1 flattens it |\n| prism | `['prism', m, at, [[a, b], ...], h, { axis }]` | an outline pushed h: up z (outline x, y), or centred along y (outline x, z) or x (outline y, z) |\n| wedge | `['wedge', m, at, [w, d, h]]` | h tall at the back, down to the floor at the front |\n| rock | `['rock', m, at, r, { sz, seed, rough }]` | a lumpy, faceted ball; below the floor is not drawn |\n| use | `['use', id, at, { rot, scale, mats, ...params }]` | another shape inside this one |\n| mirror | `['mirror', 'x' \\| 'y' \\| 'xy', part]` | the part and its mirror copies |\n| ring, row | `['ring', n, radius, part]`, `['row', n, [dx, dy, dz], part]` | n copies round z (the part moved out to radius); n copies in a line, centred |\n| group | `['group', at, rot, [parts]]` | parts moved and turned together |\n\nEvery part takes `{ rot }`: a yaw in degrees, or [x, y, z] degrees. Write arrays densely (`[0,0,8]`, not `[0, 0, 8]`):\nspaces cost a third more tokens.\n\n### Worked entries\n\n```js\nbarrel: { about: 'oak barrel, two iron hoops', tags: 'container wood', mats: { w: 'oak', h: 'iron' },\n  parts: [['lathe','w',[0,0,0],[[0,0],[5,0],[6,3],[6.5,8],[6,13],[5,16],[0,16]]], ['row',2,[0,0,9],['cyl','h',[0,0,7.5],6.5,1.5]]] },\ncrate: { about: 'wooden crate, framed and braced', tags: 'container wood', mats: { c: 'crate' }, parts: [['box','c',[0,0,0],[15,15,14]]] },\ntable: { about: 'plank table, four legs', tags: 'furniture tavern', mats: { t: 'oak', l: 'walnut' },\n  parts: [['box','t',[0,0,10],[24,14,2]], ['mirror','xy',['box','l',[10,5,0],[2,2,10]]]] },\nchest: { about: 'oak chest, round lid, iron bands', tags: 'container treasure', mats: { w: 'oak', b: 'iron', l: 'gold' },\n  parts: [['box','w',[0,0,0],[16,10,8]], ['prism','w',[0,0,8],[[-5,0],[5,0],[4,3],[0,5],[-4,3]],16,{axis:'x'}],\n    ['mirror','x',['box','b',[5.5,0,0],[1.5,11,8.5]]], ['box','l',[0,5,5],[3,1,3]]] },\nlamppost: { about: 'iron street lamp, glowing head', tags: 'town light', mats: { m: 'iron', g: 'flame' }, lights: [[0,0,40,'#ffd070',70]],\n  parts: [['cyl','m',[0,0,0],2.5,3], ['cyl','m',[0,0,3],1,34], ['box','g',[0,0,37],[4,4,5]], ['cyl','m',[0,0,42],3.5,3,{r2:0,seg:4}]] },\nfence: { about: 'rail fence, len long (16)', tags: 'village farm', mats: { w: 'oak' }, params: { len: 16 },\n  make: p => [['mirror','x',['box','w',[p.len / 2 - 1,0,0],[2,2,12]]], ['row',2,[0,0,5],['box','w',[0,0,6],[p.len - 2,1,1.5]]]] },\n```\n\nA generator seeds its noise from its params (`E.rng(seed)`), never `Math.random`, so it draws the same pixels everywhere.\n\n## Make one: the recipe\n\n1. **Read this card whole.**\n2. **Find** the nearest entry: `node tools/asset-find.mjs \"tavern table\"` searches the core library and the store's sets\n   (`src/assets/sets/`, about a thousand objects, materials and sprites converted from CC0 libraries, each with where it\n   came from) and `--show set:id` prints one; the Asset Lab (`/asset-lab`) shows them all.\n3. **Copy** it and change its names, parts and materials, or write a new one from the parts above. Give the cue a player\n   knows it by parts of its own (a skep's coils as stacked rings, an anvil's horn, a barrow's open tub dark inside), and\n   keep small parts plain ('none', 'glaze'): a busy pattern on a part a few pixels wide reads as speckle.\n4. **Look:** `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool` (a file that sets `E.SHAPES.stool`, or a store set; a core id\n   alone works too: `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs barrel`). It draws `check-output/asset-sheet/stool.png`: every view and\n   four facings, beside the hero, on a light and a dark floor. It lints what reads badly: too small from above, a part\n   under a pixel, too few tones, low contrast with the floor, its tokens. Read the picture, fix, repeat.\n5. **Use** it: `E.SHAPES.stool = {...}` and `r.shape('stool', ...)`; or a set file\n   (`(window.ASSETS = window.ASSETS || {}).MINE = { set: 'MINE', format: 1, materials, shapes }`) and\n   `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS.MINE)`, which names its entries 'MINE:stool' so they never collide, and returns the names.\n   A store set loads the same way (its script, then `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS['kenney-furniture'])`);\n   `node tools/asset-set.mjs <sets> --ids a,b --name mine --out mine.js` cuts the few a game uses, with what they need\n   (Emberdeep's set dressing loads `src/assets/sets/emberdeep.js`, cut so).\n\n## Limits\n\n- Still objects. Lids that open, swinging signs and held weapons are code (or props) for now.\n- A shape sorts by one point, as props do; a long one (a bed, a cart) may cross a body walking right beside it.\n- Collision is the footprint's box, turned with the shape, and the bounds' height: a table is solid under its top too.\n- Bigger than about 240 pixels on screen, a shape skips the actors' effects (flash, x-ray).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'maze|GridMover|Arcade|new E.Game|attract|scenes|menu:' vendor/engine/kits/arcade.js && rg -n '\"'^##|GridMover|Settings|Shell|Audio|Grid'\"\\\\.from|rig' vendor/engine/AI_GUIDE.md | head -65 && rg -n 'class Settings|class Menu|class GridMover|class Blob|function Settings|define\\\\(' vendor/engine/my-3d2dge.js | head -25\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'maze|GridMover|Arcade|new E.Game|attract|scenes|menu:' vendor/engine/kits/arcade.js && rg -n '\"'^##|GridMover|Settings|Shell|Audio|Grid'\"\\\\.from|rig' vendor/engine/AI_GUIDE.md | head -65 && rg -n 'class Settings|class Menu|class GridMover|class Blob|function Settings|define\\\\(' vendor/engine/my-3d2dge.js | head -25\"","aggregated_output":"1:// The ARCADE kit's game (a cabinet of three: maze, formation, rocks), readable: the starter's shell (src/starter/00-shell.js), then\n12: * scenes, and delete this menu and the slices you do not use; this shell (the game, its scenes, prompt, best and the\n19:/* ---- SHELL: one game, several scenes. Each slice switches view, keys and resolution on entry ----\n21:const game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'side', bg: '#07060d' });\n23:const scenes = {};\n90: * slice's run(n) is one (__game.scenes.adventure.run(600)): the same number every time ---- */\n112:scenes.title = {\n148:scenes.over = {\n162: * (C, a pad's Y, or Settings: Arcade, Look), each with its own high-score table, initials on a letter wheel, a continue,\n163: * two players taking turns, and a demo in the attract mode.\n164: *   MAZE (#arcade/maze): eat every glow-seed in a hedge maze while four wisps hunt you, each its own way (one chases, one\n166: *     them blue and edible for a while; the side tunnel wraps round. Each maze is new (E.gen's braided maze, mirrored);\n176: * HOW IT WORKS: E.Arcade (the arcade module) keeps the scores, lives, turns, tables, initials, cards, the continue and the\n177: * attract mode; the maze is an E.Grid with E.GridMovers (a turn asked early is kept), each game a { begin, step, draw }.\n182:  game.settings.define({ key: 'arcadeLook', group: 'Arcade', label: 'Look', options: [['remaster', 'Remaster'], ['classic', 'Classic']], value: 'remaster', help: 'Remaster: lit, with HD rigs and glowi [... omitted end of long line]\n232:    // a new maze: the left half from E.gen's braided maze, mirrored; doors in the middle wall; the wisps' house in a ring\n233:    // of hall; the tunnel's ends opened. The same level gives the same maze\n235:      const half = E.gen.maze(15, MH, { seed: 7 + level * 13, braid: 1 }).rows;\n250:      hero = { m: new E.GridMover({ cx: start[0], cy: start[1], speed: 7.5 }), dir: [-1, 0], chomp: 0, dying: 0 };\n253:      wisps = WCOL.map((c, i) => ({ i, c, m: new E.GridMover({ cx: i ? 10 + i * 2 : DOOR[0], cy: i ? HY + 2 : DOOR[1], speed: 6.8 }), home: i > 0, wait: i * 2.5, eyes: false, leaving: false, blue: fal [... omitted end of long line]\n315:    // after the death's turn: a life gone; the other player's maze (two players, each keeps theirs), or the end\n327:    // the demo's player (the attract mode, and tools/kits-test.mjs): it counts the steps to every cell, its own and the\n362:      // the maze on the floor (lit in the remaster: hedges with a sunlit top edge and a shadow at the foot; the classic's\n416:      id: 'maze', label: 'MAZE', res: [232, 270], about: 'EAT EVERY SEED, DODGE FOUR WISPS', hue: ['#ffe040', '#4ae8ff', '#ff4a8a'], touch: [], extra: [10000], light: .5,\n418:      revive() { over = false; reset(); },   // after a continue: the same maze, everyone back in place\n631:  /* =================================== 4. THE CABINET: a scene per game, the title, the attract mode =================================== */\n633:  const cabs = {}; for (const G of GAMES) cabs[G.id] = new E.Arcade(game, { id: G.id, lives: 3, extra: G.extra || [], every: G.every });\n634:  const front = new E.Arcade(game, { id: 'arcade', idle: 20, demo: 1e9, scores: 6 });   // the title's attract clock (a demo ends itself: endDemo)\n679:  for (const G of GAMES) scenes['arcade-' + G.id] = playScene(G);\n687:      if (d && d.part && scenes['arcade-' + d.part]) game.go('arcade-' + d.part, null, { fade: 0 });   // #arcade/maze\n690:      const I = game.input, ph = front.attract(dt);\n738:     *  five minutes of game time, or its lives run out); then the attract mode left alone: the demo it starts, the table, the title again. What they reached */\n752:        out.attract = demoAt.startsWith('arcade-') && back === 'arcade' && table && front.phase === 'title';\n757:     *  screen, the attract mode's phase */\n760:    run: (n = 600) => GAMES.map(G => scenes['arcade-' + G.id].run(n)).join('-')\n764:scenes.arcade = ARCADE;\n768:for (let i = SLICES.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) if (!scenes[SLICES[i].id]) SLICES.splice(i, 1);\n770:// #<slice>/<part> jumps in (#arcade/maze, #shooter/horizontal): the slice's enter(data) gets { part }; a part that is a\n772:const [first, part] = (location.hash || '').slice(1).split('/'), sc = scenes[first], to = sc && part && sc.parts && sc.parts[part];\n773:game.start({ scenes, scene: to || (sc ? first : 'title'), data: sc && part ? { part } : undefined });\n774:window.__game = { game, scenes, titleMenu };   // for tests\n7:## North star\n17:## The mental model\n29:## Remake workflow (port, remaster, reimagine, remix)\n34:4. **Rebuild the content.** Levels are ASCII rows with a legend, enemies are small factories, items are sprites. Keep the original's layout ideas but make your own maps; for a remix, change the rules  [... omitted end of long line]\n41:6. **Tune the feel** with the feel numbers in `API.md`, then check the game with the skeleton overlay (`rig.debug(r)`) and in slow motion (`game.timeScale = .25`).\n44:## Visual craft: aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES\n53:- *NES typography.* Use the default 5x7 font with a drop shadow for HUD text, `E.ui.box` gradient panels and bars, and `E.font.title` with a 3-4 color gradient and extrusion for logos. A modern adapta [... omitted end of long line]\n54:- *Faceless dialog.* Every talking character gets a portrait: `talk.say(lines, { name, portrait: rig })`, and `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 40)` on HUDs and RPG party and status screens.\n60:- *Legend of Mana / Secret of Mana:* the `threequarter` view, `E.tex.grass` / `dirt` / `water` floors by floor tag (a river with `block: true` and a plank bridge), houses of `timber` or `plaster` wall [... omitted end of long line]\n61:- *Klonoa and 2.5D platformers:* PlatformMap in the `brawler` view (tiles show their top faces), a bright `'day'` or `'forest'` backdrop, props in front of and behind the play plane (`y` of -12 and +1 [... omitted end of long line]\n64:## Animation: fluid, varied, readable\n65:Broken animation ruins a good-looking game faster than flat art. The Humanoid rig animates procedurally, so use its vocabulary instead of moving joints by hand:\n72:## Hard rules\n82:## Standard features (built in: adjust them, don't rebuild them)\n84:- **Pause menu** (full engine): a pausable scene pauses on Escape, P, Start, the touch START button or the corner's menu button, and shows Resume, your items, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help (wh [... omitted end of long line]\n85:- **Settings** (`game.settings`): Display (full screen; pixel scaling for a fixed `res`: Auto, Sharp, Fill; a screen filter: Clean, Scanlines or CRT, drawn as the picture reaches the canvas, never on  [... omitted end of long line]\n86:- **Controls**: every action of the current preset, rebound by pressing the new key, mouse button or gamepad button (a conflict is said and nothing changes; Escape, Enter, the F keys, Start, the arrow [... omitted end of long line]\n87:- **Full screen** (`game.fullscreen`): a phone's first tap goes full screen, the top-right button and the menu toggle it, and once the player leaves it the game never forces it back. Where a browser c [... omitted end of long line]\n89:- **The corner**: a menu button and a full-screen button at the top right, inside the safe area. They show on titles, game overs and pauses, for a moment when the mouse nears the top, and the menu sta [... omitted end of long line]\n110:- Your own `E.Shell` takes over from the engine's and keeps the standard menu (`standard: false` leaves it out).\n120:## Frame order\n140:## Systems in more depth\n142:### Views and resolution\n146:- `res` fixes the internal resolution with whole-number scaling. Without it, the size adapts to the window between `minH` and `maxW` × `maxH`; `portrait: { maxH }` lets the view grow on a screen held  [... omitted end of long line]\n147:- `res` with `expand: true` (what the starter kits use) fills any window with no bars: `res` is the least the screen shows, and `r.W` × `r.H` grows on the long side (a 16:9 monitor shows about 427×240 [... omitted end of long line]\n150:### Scenes\n155:### Camera\n159:- The player's zoom: a scene's `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5 }` sets how close the camera starts (`start`; `tall` on a phone held upright) and how far the player may take it (`zoom`): [... omitted end of long line]\n163:### Players (two to four on one machine)\n165:- `game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin: (input, n) => dropIn(input) })` in a scene's `enter` lets them in: Start on a gamepad no one holds, or 2 on the keyboard. Two on one keyboard each get half: player  [... omitted end of long line]\n169:### Motion and timing (tweens, scripts, paths)\n178:### TileMap (ground-plane levels)\n190:### Heights: roofs, ledges, sight and reach\n222:### PlatformMap (side-scrolling levels)\n226:  - `slope`: `dir: 1` rises to the right, `-1` to the left, `from` / `to` (0..1) for gentle slopes; bodies walk up and down them and step up onto low ledges;\n236:  - `hitWall`: -1 left, 1 right, 0 none;\n242:### Platformer (side-scroller controller)\n247:- `rigState(extra)` returns the object `Humanoid.update` wants, including the airborne pose.\n249:### Body (ground-plane physics)\n255:### Mover and actions (third-person ground controller)\n261:- Gliding (`glide: true`, Breath of the Wild, Arkham): a jump press in the air, `glideMin` (24) over the ground, opens a sail: it sinks at `glideSink`, flies at `glideSpeed`, takes no fall damage, ris [... omitted end of long line]\n264:- `mover.rigState(extra)` feeds `rig.update`: its crouch (a sneaking gait), sprint lean, air, landing, or the playing action's pose.\n265:- Actions (`E.ACTIONS`): `climbUp` (played backwards to climb down), `vault`, `hopDown`, `roll` (invulnerable in its middle), `getUp`, and `takedown`, a paired action that moves two bodies: `mover.pla [... omitted end of long line]\n267:### Perception (sight, hearing, suspicion)\n273:### Attack (melee timing)\n276:- `hits(targets, test, fn)` calls `fn` once per target per swing while active. `state` feeds `rig.update({ attack })`.\n278:- **Special moves** (input sequences): `game.input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing: hero.facing })` is true on the step a fireball's motion ends: down, down-forward, forward, then the button (numpad  [... omitted end of long line]\n280:### Bullets and patterns\n286:### Humanoid\n294:  - `spin: true` swings a full circle, `kick: true` swings the right foot, `blade: 0` hides the smear.\n295:- `hand()` and `tip()` give world points for spawning bullets or sparks; `worldOffset(p)` gives where any point in the rig's own frame (a joint in `J`) sits from its feet. `debug(r)` draws the skeleto [... omitted end of long line]\n297:### Audio\n305:### Text and UI\n311:### Shell (an HTML interface: screens and HUD)\n312:A modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild) wants readable HTML screens over the pixel world, not pixel windows. `E.Shell` builds them from data, so you write none of the HTML, CSS or f [... omitted end of long line]\n313:- `const ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme: { ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font, titleFont }, settings: { items: [{ key: 'cones', label: 'Sight cones', value: true }], onChange(s, key) {} },  [... omitted end of long line]\n314:- Screens: `ui.show(name, spec)`, `ui.back()`, `ui.hide()`, `ui.screen` (the open one's name, or null). Every spec takes `kicker`, `title`, `text` (strings or functions), `actions: [[label, fn], { lab [... omitted end of long line]\n316:- Input: the game's own input moves the focus (keys, d-pad, stick: up / down / left / right, a slider or a choice turns left and right), confirm presses, cancel goes back, pause closes the menu; touch [... omitted end of long line]\n319:### Lighting\n328:### Errors and warnings\n338:### Testing without a browser\n342:- Input: `game.input.tap('jump')` presses an action for one step (`pressed` and `down` in the next step, `released` in the one after); `hold('right')` holds it until `hold('right', false)`; `press(cod [... omitted end of long line]\n357:### The handoff package\n358:`dist/handoff/` (on the site: `/handoff/`; `node tools/handoff.mjs --zip <file>` zips it for a chat) holds what a coding agent needs to build on the full engine without the repository: both editions,  [... omitted end of long line]\n360:### Modules (general systems in files of their own)\n3087:class Settings {\n3104:    for (const k in STANDARD_SETTINGS) if (!(game.o.touch === 'never' && STANDARD_SETTINGS[k].group === 'Touch')) this.define(Object.assign({ key: k }, STANDARD_SETTINGS[k]));   // (touch: 'never': no [... omitted end of long line]\n3105:    for (const sp of own) this.define(sp && !sp.label && Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(STANDARD_SETTINGS, sp.key) ? Object.assign({ key: sp.key }, STANDARD_SETTINGS[sp.key], sp) : sp);   // (a  [... omitted end of long line]\n3110:  define(sp) {\n3772:  if (!this.settings.spec('playerBindings')) this.settings.define({ key: 'playerBindings', group: 'Controls', type: 'bindings', value: () => ({}), apply: (g, v) => { for (const p of g.players.slice(1) [... omitted end of long line]\n5038:class Blob {\n7745:    /** presets: the named sound effects, sfx(name) plays one and define(name, voice) adds one; song: the music\n7842:    if (!p) { warn('sfx:' + what, 'unknown sound \"' + what + '\". Presets: ' + Object.keys(this.presets).join(', ') + ' (or audio.define(name, voice))'); return H; }\n7860:  /** add or replace a named sound: audio.define('zap', { wave: 'saw', freq: 1200, to: 200, dur: .2 }) */\n7861:  define(name, voice) { this.presets[name] = voice; }\n9269:class Menu {\n9435:    for (const it of this._items()) if (!S.spec(it.key)) { if (S._raw[it.key] === undefined && old[it.key] !== undefined) S._raw[it.key] = old[it.key]; S.define(Object.assign({ group: 'Game' }, it));  [... omitted end of long line]\n12462:class GridMover {\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
